      "latency_ms": 15051.7,
      "prompt_tokens": 28,
      "completion_tokens": 1192,
      "output": "**Water** is a transparent, tasteless, odorless, and nearly colorless chemical substance that is the main constituent of Earth's hydrosphere and the fluids of all known living organisms. It is essential for all known forms of life.\n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is from chemical, physical, biological, and planetary perspectives:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Chemical Identity\n*   **Molecular Formula:** **H₂O** (two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom).\n*   **Molecular Structure:** The molecule is **bent (V-shaped)** at an angle of ~104.5°. Because oxygen is more electronegative than hydrogen, the molecule is **polar**—it has a partial negative charge near the oxygen and partial positive charges near the hydrogens.\n*   **Hydrogen Bonding:** This polarity allows water molecules to stick to one another via **hydrogen bonds**. This relatively weak but abundant intermolecular force is responsible for almost all of water’s unique properties.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Unique Physical Properties (Caused by Hydrogen Bonding)\nWater behaves differently than almost any other substance on Earth:\n\n| Property | Description | Why It Matters |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Universal Solvent** | Dissolves more substances than any other liquid. | Transports nutrients in blood, shapes geology (caves, canyons), enables cellular chemistry. |\n| **Density Anomaly** | **Ice floats.** Water is densest at 4°C (39°F); it expands when freezing. | Insulates lakes/oceans in winter, allowing life to survive underneath. |\n| **High Specific Heat Capacity** | Absorbs a lot of heat before temperature rises. | Stabilizes Earth’s climate; regulates body temperature in organisms. |\n| **High Heat of Vaporization** | Requires massive energy to turn into gas. | Sweating/evaporation cools organisms efficiently. |\n| **Cohesion & Adhesion** | Sticks to itself (cohesion) and other surfaces (adhesion). | **Capillary action** allows water to move up tree roots and through blood vessels. |\n| **Surface Tension** | High surface tension creates a \"skin\" on the surface. | Allows insects (water striders) to walk on water. |\n\n---\n\n### 3. The Three States of Matter (on Earth)\nWater is the only common substance to exist naturally in all three states within Earth's normal temperature range:\n1.  **Solid (Ice):** Crystalline structure (hexagonal), less dense than liquid.\n2.  **Liquid (Water):** The standard state for biology and hydrology (0°C to 100°C at 1 atm).\n3.  **Gas (Water Vapor/Steam):** Invisible gas; a potent **greenhouse gas** critical for climate regulation.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Biological Role: The \"Matrix of Life\"\n*   **Solvent for Metabolism:** Dissolves ions, proteins, DNA, and sugars so chemical reactions can occur.\n*   **Reactant:** Participates directly in **hydrolysis** (breaking bonds) and **photosynthesis** (6CO₂ + 6H₂O → C₆H₁₂O₆ + 6O₂).\n*   **Structural Support:** **Turgor pressure** in plant cells keeps them rigid; hydrostatic skeletons in worms/jellyfish.\n*   **Transport Medium:** Blood (plasma is ~90% water), lymph, sap in plants.\n*   **Temperature Buffer:** High heat capacity prevents rapid temperature swings in cells.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Planetary Distribution (The Hydrosphere)\n*   **~71% of Earth's surface** is covered by water.\n*   **97.5% is Saline** (oceans).\n*   **2.5% is Freshwater**, of which:\n    *   ~68.7% is locked in **ice caps/glaciers**.\n    *   ~30.1% is **groundwater**.\n    *   Only **~0.3% is surface water** (lakes, rivers, swamps) — the primary source for human use.\n*   **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Continuous movement via evaporation, transpiration, condensation, precipitation, and runoff.\n\n---\n\n### 6. Human Context\n*   **Physiology:** The adult human body is **~60% water** (brain ~73%, lungs ~83%). We survive only **~3 days** without it.\n*   **Agriculture:** ~70% of global freshwater withdrawals go to irrigation.\n*   **Industry/Energy:** Cooling for power plants (thermal/nuclear), manufacturing, hydroelectric power.\n*   **Scarcity:** Despite abundance, **clean, accessible freshwater is a finite, stressed resource** due to pollution, climate change, and over-extraction.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Definition\n> **Water (H₂O) is a small, polar molecule whose hydrogen bonding creates anomalous physical properties—most notably a solid phase less dense than its liquid phase and a high capacity to store heat and dissolve matter. These properties make it the universal solvent, the primary driver of Earth's climate, and the indispensable medium for all known biochemistry.**"
